<!-- K-FACTOR / REFERRAL MATH — output becomes input, a closing ring -->
<section class="wl-kfactor" aria-label="The math of compounding without paid acquisition">
  <header class="wl-kfactor-header">
    <span class="wl-kfactor-eyebrow reveal">The math</span>
    <h3 class="wl-kfactor-heading reveal">A loop, not a funnel.</h3>
    <p class="wl-kfactor-lede reveal">Each user became an inviter. The math only widened.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="wl-kfactor-ring reveal">
    <svg class="wl-kfactor-svg" viewBox="0 0 400 400" role="img" aria-label="A closed loop: 1 user becomes 7 invites, then 49, 343, and 2,401-plus, which feeds back into the first user. The output becomes the input. At the center, the real result: 10,000-plus users from one referrer, 3 million signups, zero paid acquisition.">
      <defs>
        <!-- arrowhead piercing node 1; uses currentColor so the marker inherits the rose set on the element -->
        <marker id="wlReentryHead" viewBox="0 0 12 12" refX="8.5" refY="6" markerWidth="16" markerHeight="16" orient="auto-start-reverse" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <path d="M1 1 L11 6 L1 11 Z" fill="currentColor" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- base ring — faint, the circuit the progression rides -->
      <circle class="wl-kfactor-base" cx="200" cy="200" r="120" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-white-12)" stroke-width="1.5" />

      <!-- main draw-on ring: clockwise from node 1 (top) round to node 5 (top-left) -->
      <!-- arc sweeps the 4 progression gaps; the rose re-entry arc closes the final gap -->
      <path class="wl-kfactor-track"
            d="M 200 80
               A 120 120 0 1 1 85.87 162.92"
            pathLength="1"
            fill="none" stroke="var(--color-white-25)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" />

      <!-- BOLD rose re-entry arc: from node 5 (2,401+) sweeping clockwise back INTO node 1 (1) -->
      <g class="wl-kfactor-reentry">
        <path class="wl-kfactor-reentry-path"
              d="M 85.87 162.92
                 A 120 120 0 0 1 187.46 80.66"
              pathLength="1"
              fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"
              marker-end="url(#wlReentryHead)" />
      </g>

      <!-- node dots, sitting on the ring -->
      <circle class="wl-kfactor-dot" data-n="0" cx="200"    cy="80"     r="5" />
      <circle class="wl-kfactor-dot" data-n="1" cx="314.13" cy="162.92" r="5" />
      <circle class="wl-kfactor-dot" data-n="2" cx="270.53" cy="297.08" r="5" />
      <circle class="wl-kfactor-dot" data-n="3" cx="129.47" cy="297.08" r="5" />
      <circle class="wl-kfactor-dot wl-kfactor-dot--terminal" data-n="4" cx="85.87" cy="162.92" r="6" />
    </svg>

    <!-- numbers + labels, positioned just OUTSIDE the ring at each node (described by the SVG label above).
         Coordinates: center 50/50, radius factor 0.46 of the box → sits clear of the 0.375 ring stroke. -->
    <span class="wl-kfactor-node wl-kfactor-node--1" aria-hidden="true" style="--x:50%;--y:9%;">
      <b class="wl-kfactor-num">1</b>
      <em class="wl-kfactor-cap">user</em>
    </span>
    <span class="wl-kfactor-node wl-kfactor-node--2" aria-hidden="true" style="--x:87%;--y:37.5%;">
      <b class="wl-kfactor-num">7</b>
      <em class="wl-kfactor-cap">invites</em>
    </span>
    <span class="wl-kfactor-node wl-kfactor-node--3" aria-hidden="true" style="--x:74%;--y:83%;">
      <b class="wl-kfactor-num">49</b>
      <em class="wl-kfactor-cap">2nd degree</em>
    </span>
    <span class="wl-kfactor-node wl-kfactor-node--4" aria-hidden="true" style="--x:26%;--y:83%;">
      <b class="wl-kfactor-num">343</b>
      <em class="wl-kfactor-cap">3rd degree</em>
    </span>
    <span class="wl-kfactor-node wl-kfactor-node--5" aria-hidden="true" style="--x:11%;--y:37.5%;">
      <b class="wl-kfactor-num wl-kfactor-num--terminal">2,401+</b>
      <em class="wl-kfactor-cap wl-kfactor-cap--terminal">five rounds in</em>
    </span>

    <!-- gravitational core: the real production proof the model produced -->
    <div class="wl-kfactor-core" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="wl-kfactor-core-stat">4M+</span>
      <span class="wl-kfactor-core-sub">signups · zero paid acquisition</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STAGE THREE -->
<section class="sub-section sub-section--editorial">
  <div class="sub-section-inner">
    <span class="sub-section-label">Stage three — Post-launch</span>
    <div>
      <h2 class="sub-section-heading">Cash.<br/>Routed back to <span class="accent">the users</span>.</h2>
      <div class="sub-section-body">
        <p>Status worked pre-launch because we had nothing else to offer. Post-launch, with users transacting, the reward unit had to switch to cash. We paid Rs. 1,000 per SadaBiz freelancer referral.</p>
        <p>The economic model was clean. SadaBiz users earned dollars from international clients, which earned the company real revenue. Rather than spend it on social ads, we routed it back to users referring more freelancers. More invites → more SadaBiz users → more company earnings → more rewards to users. The marketing budget became the user.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<figure class="wl-shot wl-shot--single">
  <img src="/images/Sadapay/refer-freelancers.png" alt="SadaPay SadaBiz referral screen. Green illustration at top. Heading reads Refer freelancers, earn Rs. 1,000. Subtext: They join SadaBiz. You get rewarded." class="wl-shot-img" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption class="wl-shot-caption">Steered deliberately toward SadaBiz freelancers — the highest-LTV users we had.</figcaption>
</figure>

<!-- OUTCOME -->
<section class="sub-section sub-section--editorial">
  <div class="sub-section-inner">
    <span class="sub-section-label">Outcome</span>
    <div>
      <h2 class="sub-section-heading">Five million users.<br/>Through one <span class="accent">designed funnel</span>.</h2>
      <div class="sub-section-body">
        <p>Five million users came through the same status → scarcity → cash system between 2021 and 2023, processing $1 billion a year, with the cash stage steering the dollar-earning freelancers toward that volume. We became one of the two that reached scale, by designing demand instead of buying it. SadaPay sold to Papara for up to $50M in May 2024. After I left, people still introduce themselves to me with <em>"I hold a Founders Club card."</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

